```tikz
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.2, transform shape]
    % Define the coordinates of the parallelogram vertices
    \coordinate (A) at (0,0);
    \coordinate (B) at (4,0);
    \coordinate (C) at (5,2);
    \coordinate (D) at (1,2);

    % Draw the parallelogram
    \draw[thick, line join=round] (A) -- (B) -- (C) -- (D) -- cycle;

    % Draw the diagonal
    \draw[thick, line join=round, dashed] (A) -- (C);

    % Label the vertices
    \node[below left] at (A) {A};
    \node[below right] at (B) {B};
    \node[above right] at (C) {C};
    \node[above left] at (D) {D};

    % Label the sides
    \node[midway, below] at (A) -- (B) {AB};
    \node[midway, right] at (B) -- (C) {BC};
    \node[midway, above] at (C) -- (D) {CD};
    \node[midway, left] at (D) -- (A) {DA};

    % Label the angles
    \node[below left] at (A) {$\angle A$};
    \node[below right] at (B) {$\angle B$};
    \node[above right] at (C) {$\angle C$};
    \node[above left] at (D) {$\angle D$};

    % Label the diagonal
    \node[midway, above left] at (A) -- (C) {AC};
\end{tikzpicture}
```

### Explanation

- [h]Theorem[/h]: In a parallelogram, a diagonal bisects the parallelogram into two congruent triangles.
- [c]A[/c] and [c]C[/c] are opposite vertices of the parallelogram.
- [u]Diagonal AC[/u] divides the parallelogram into two triangles, $\triangle ABC$ and $\triangle ADC$.
- [b]Congruent Triangles[/b]: $\triangle ABC \cong \triangle ADC$ because:
  - [s]AB = DC[/s] (opposite sides of a parallelogram are equal)
  - [s]AD = BC[/s] (opposite sides of a parallelogram are equal)
  - [s]AC = AC[/s] (common side)
- Therefore, the diagonal [u]AC[/u] bisects the parallelogram into two congruent triangles.

